short answer: `{ a: 1 }` creates a brand new object every time the parent renders. React compares props with `Object.is`, so the new reference counts as changed even when the contents are identical.

also, a child re-renders whenever its parent does, props or not. the prop comparison only matters if the child is wrapped in `React.memo` (or is a `PureComponent`). if you memoized it and it still re-renders, the object literal is what's breaking it.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ style }) {
  return <div style={style} />;
});

function Parent() {
  return <Child style={{ color: "red" }} />;
}
```

fixes, pick whichever fits:

1. if the object is static, hoist it out of the component so it's created once:

```jsx
const style = { color: "red" };

function Parent() {
  return <Child style={style} />;
}
```

2. if it depends on state or props, wrap it in `useMemo`:

```jsx
const style = useMemo(() => ({ color }), [color]);
```

3. pass primitives instead (`color={color}`) so the comparison works naturally.

same deal for inline functions and arrays, they're new references each render too (`useCallback` for functions).

if you're on the React Compiler, it memoizes this kind of thing automatically, so you'd only see it without the compiler enabled.

it's a new object every render. `{a: 1} === {a: 1}` is `false` in js because objects compare by reference, not by contents. each time the parent renders, the literal gets built again at a new memory address, so the prop is "different" even though the values are identical. think two keys cut from the same pattern: same shape, but not the same key.

also, if the child isn't wrapped in `React.memo`, it re-renders whenever the parent does, props or no props. memo only helps if the props are referentially stable, and an inline object literal breaks that.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ style }) {
  return <div style={style} />;
});

function Parent() {
  return <Child style={{ color: "red" }} />;
}
```

this re-renders `Child` every time because `style` is a fresh object.

fixes, pick one:

if the object never changes, hoist it out of the component:

```jsx
const style = { color: "red" };

function Parent() {
  return <Child style={style} />;
}
```

if it depends on props or state, memoize it:

```jsx
function Parent({ color }) {
  const style = useMemo(() => ({ color }), [color]);
  return <Child style={style} />;
}
```

or pass primitives (`color="red"`) so the shallow compare works on its own.

same thing happens with inline arrays and functions (`onClick={() => ...}`), those need `useCallback` for stability.

if you're on the React Compiler, it handles most of this automatically, but only for components it successfully compiles.

